Kernir patch 0012: zip comments, raw bytes (entry-comment reader accessor and writer hook, archive-comment writer)

Upstream: libarchive 3.8.10, libarchive/archive_read_support_format_zip.c (struct zip_entry,
archive_read_format_zip_seekable_bid, slurp_central_directory, cleanup), libarchive/archive_write.c
(_archive_write_header, _archive_write_free), libarchive/archive_write_private.h (struct archive_write) and libarchive/archive_write_set_format_zip.c
(struct zip, archive_write_zip_header, archive_write_zip_finish_entry, archive_write_zip_close,
archive_write_zip_free). Declarations in include/kernir_names.h (added by 0009). Applies on top of 0009.

Upstream reads every zip comment length and skips the bytes, and its writer writes neither entry comments
(central-directory comment length 0) nor an archive comment (end-of-central-directory comment length 0), so a
repack through libarchive drops both. Kernir copies them zip -> zip, byte for byte, with no charset conversion.

Reader: slurp_central_directory() keeps each central-directory record's comment bytes in its zip_entry
(freed in cleanup). kernir_read_zip_entry_comment(a, &bytes, &length) returns 1 with the current entry's
comment (length 0: none) when the seekable reader read it from the central directory, 0 for the streaming
reader (no central directory) or when there is no current entry, and -1 when the archive is not being read
as zip.

Reader, end-record search: upstream's seekable bidder looks for the end-of-central-directory record in the
last 16 KiB only, so behind an archive comment over about 16 KiB (the format allows 65 535 bytes, Kernir's
editor 16 000 characters) the streaming reader opens the zip and every entry comment is lost.
archive_read_format_zip_seekable_bid() now reads the last 20 + 22 + 65 535 bytes (zip64 locator, end record,
longest comment). A signature in the last 16 KiB is taken by upstream's rule, unchanged (the same candidate,
no comment-length check). A signature further from EOF is taken only when 22 + its comment length reaches
EOF exactly; others are skipped and the search goes on. The record found is checked as before (read_eocd, and
the zip64 locator 20 bytes before it, which the wider window now always contains), so 0011 still applies.
A zip with more than 16 KiB of data after an end record without a matching comment is still not taken.

Writer, entry comment: kernir_write_zip_next_comment(a, bytes, length) (at most 0xffff bytes; 0 clears)
arms the next archive_write_header() only. The request is kept in struct archive_write and taken by
_archive_write_header() before anything else, next to 0009's raw-name request, so it is used up by the next
header whether that header succeeds, fails in the zip writer, or returns early before the format runs.
archive_write_zip_header() moves the taken bytes into the zip writer before any return (the previous entry
was already finished); archive_write_zip_finish_entry() appends them to the central-directory record after
the extra fields (UT, zip64), once the extra length is fixed, and sets the record's comment length.

Writer, archive comment: kernir_write_zip_archive_comment(a, bytes, length) (at most 0xffff bytes; 0: none;
any time before close, a later call replaces it). archive_write_zip_close() writes its length into the end
record and the bytes right after it; zip64 end records come before the end record, so they are unchanged.

Both writer calls return ARCHIVE_FATAL (and set an error) when the handle is not a zip writer or the length
is over 0xffff. Without the calls the reader and writer write exactly what upstream writes.

Apply from Sources/CLibArchive: patch -p1 < patches/0012-zip-comments.patch

